
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Tracking only — that removes the security-critical dimension and keeps this much smaller. It stays architectural
  (still a shared module, storage, and future consumers), but the stakes are now design cleanliness rather than access
  control.

  Next question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the id actually identifies, which decides its lifetime and its storage.

  - [1mPer-login session id[0m — a fresh opaque id minted on each successful login. Answers "which login session produced this
    log line." Clean privacy story, but you can't connect two sessions by the same person.
  - [1mStable per-browser id[0m — generated once, survives logins and browser restarts. Answers "is this the same person
    coming back," which is usually what "track who logged in" turns out to mean. Can't tell two people apart on a shared
    browser unless you also record the username.
  - [1mThe username itself[0m — literally "who," human-readable in logs, zero generation logic. But it puts PII into storage
    and every log line, and it breaks as an identifier if a username ever changes.

  Cheap to change later: the generation logic. Expensive: the semantics — every future form and every log line already
  written assumes one meaning, and switching means your historical data no longer lines up.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the stable per-browser id. It matches the intent behind "track who logged in" across sessions,
  keeps PII out of storage, and a per-login id can be layered on top later as a second field without invalidating
  anything.
